  ],
  "slug": "serum-extracellular-b51d",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and papers test whether imputation, harmonization and cross-cohort metrics yield conclusions that survive changes in data source.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is validation of computational inference in biomedical data: whether models, imputation pipelines, and integration methods produce conclusions that hold beyond the setting in which they were fitted. Several works show that similar aggregate performance can mask divergent behaviour, as with ICU sepsis imputation pipelines that reach similar AUROC while flagging different patients under alert-list concordance. Others show that cross-cohort evaluation is distorted by label-space mismatch in breast cancer subtyping, that reproducibility alone does not validate SABLE-MO latent factors, and that harmonization choice matters, with ComBat leading among 2,234 approaches on B-cell lymphoma transcriptomes. Donor-sensitive state boundaries in rheumatoid arthritis B cells and evidence bounds for transcript-derived serine metabolic scores point the same way. The new element is the explicit use of concordance, held-out and patient-level tests as the unit of evaluation rather than headline metrics.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-18"
}
